DNS amplification is a type of denial-of-service (DoS) attack. In a DNS amplification attack, an attacker sends a carefully crafted request to a vulnerable DNS server. The DNS server then sends a much larger response to a target IP address specified in the attacker's request. The large response floods the target with traffic, potentially causing a denial-of-service condition.

To protect against DNS amplification attacks, you can use the following BIND configuration options:

1. limit-query-rate
This option limits the rate at which the server responds to queries. You can set a global limit for the entire server, or a per-zone limit that applies only to queries for that zone.
To set a global limit:
```
limit-query-rate 1000;
```
To set a per-zone limit:
```
zone "<your_zone>" {
 limit-query-rate 500;
};
```
2. query-time-out
This option sets a time limit for how long the server will accept queries. After this time limit, the server will stop responding to queries.
You can set a global time limit for the entire server, or a per-zone time limit that applies only to queries for that zone.
To set a global time limit:
```
query-time-out 10;
```
To set a per-zone time limit:
```
zone "<your_zone>" {
 query-time-out 5;
};
```
3. allow-transfer
This option allows zone transfers only from specific IP addresses.
You can set this option globally or per zone. To set it per zone, replace "<your_zone>" with the name of the zone.
```
zone "<your_zone>" {
 allow-transfer { <IP_address_1> <IP_address_2> };
};
```The following code shows an example BIND configuration file that includes these options.
```
options {
 limit-query-rate 1000;
 query-time-out 10;
};
zone "<your_zone>" {
 limit-query-rate 500;
 query-time-out 5;
 allow-transfer { <IP_address_1> <IP_address_2> };
};
```To use this configuration file, save it to a file and load it into BIND using the following command:
```
$ su - cnrs -l dns -p /etc/bind/named.conf.local < /path/to/configuration/file
```Replace `<path/to/configuration/file>` with the path to the configuration file.
You can also test your BIND configuration using the `dns-online-tool` website. The website can simulate DNS queries and show you how your server responds. To use the website, visit the following URL and enter your server's IP address and domain name:
```
https://www.dns-online.com/query.html
```The website will return a list of DNS records that your server responds with. You can use this list to check whether your server is vulnerable to DNS amplification attacks.
For each record in the list, look up the corresponding IP address using a website such as `ipaddr.com`. If any of these IP addresses are larger than your server's IP address, then your server is vulnerable to DNS amplification attacks.

